'''''Spergularia media''''' ([[Synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''S. maritima'') is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Caryophyllaceae]] known by the common names '''media sandspurry'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SPMA10|taxon=Spergularia maritima|accessdate=25 November 2015}}</ref> and '''greater sea-spurrey'''.<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> It is native to Eurasia and the [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]], where it grows in many types of habitat, including disturbed areas, including places with [[soil salinity|saline substrates]], such as [[salt marsh]]es and beaches. It is known in many other parts of the world as an [[introduced species]] and a common roadside [[weed]]. In North America it is a "highway [[halophyte]]", often springing up at the margins of roads that are heavily [[road salt|salted]] in the winter.<ref>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242000841 Flora of North America]</ref>

==Description==
It is an annual or perennial herb producing a narrow stem lined with fleshy linear leaves. The small flowers are 8 to 12&nbsp;mm across with five petals which are usually slightly longer than the [[sepal]]s.<ref name="Parnell 12">Parnell, P. and Curtis, T. 2012. ''Webb's An Irish Flora''. Cork University Press {{ISBN|978-185918-4783}}</ref> The five petals are oval white or pink-tinged. The tiny seeds have winged margins. It has 10 [[stamen]]s.<ref name="Parnell 12"/><ref>Clapham, A.R., Tutin, T.G. and Warburg. 1968. ''Excursion Flora of the British Isles.'' Cambridge University Press. {{ISBN|0-521-04656-4}}</ref>

